\description{
This function grafts missing taxa onto an ultrametric, time-calibrated backbone phylogeny.
The position of each taxon is constrained by its taxonomic assignment (the target clade, defined in \code{Target_clade}),
and optionally by exclusions based on species attributes (the excluded subclades, defined in \code{Excluded_subclades}).
The divergence times of the grafted branches are either drawn uniformly along the branches (\code{method = "uniform"}),
or drawn from a birth-death model fitted on the target clade (\code{method = "BD_clades"}, default).
The backbone is never modified: only new tips are added.
}
\details{
The function stops, before any grafting, if the backbone is not ultrametric, or if some taxa have no branch available for grafting
(i.e., all branches of their target clade are excluded). The taxa concerned are listed in the error message,
so the constraints (\code{include_target_clade_stem}, \code{Excluded_subclades} or \code{exclude_subclades_stem}) can be adjusted.
With \code{method = "BD_clades"}, the function can also stop during grafting if no available branch crosses the divergence age drawn for a taxon.
\subsection{Target clades, stem regions and excluded subclades}{

Each taxon is grafted within its target clade. The branches available for grafting are all the branches of the target clade
(descending from its MRCA node), plus its stem region if the stem is available for this taxon (\code{missing_taxa_list$include_target_clade_stem} of the taxon if provided, otherwise the global argument \code{include_target_clade_stem}).
Grafting on the stem places the taxon sister to the target clade.
Taxa of the same target clade can therefore differ in their stem availability: the clade is the same (same MRCA node, same richness information),
but the branches and the time window available for the divergence times of these taxa differ.

The stem region is not restricted to the original stem branch. A taxon grafted on the stem splits it in two pieces, and becomes itself part of the stem region:
later taxa can be grafted on both pieces of the stem, and on the terminal branch of the earlier taxon (the two taxa are then sister, and together sister to the target clade).
More generally, the stem region includes all the branches of the 'missing-only subtrees' hanging from the stem, i.e., subtrees containing only taxa of \code{missing_taxa_list$Taxon_name},
whatever their own target clade. This mirrors what happens within the crown group, where missing taxa can already be grafted on the branches of other missing taxa.

Branches inside excluded subclades are removed from the pool of available branches. By default (\code{exclude_subclades_stem = TRUE}),
the stem region of the excluded subclades is removed as well: the taxon can be neither nested within the excluded subclade,
nor sister to it (nor sister to the missing taxa already grafted on its stem).
This is the only way to exclude a single tip, as its terminal branch is its only branch.
With \code{exclude_subclades_stem = FALSE} (globally, or for a given subclade in \code{missing_taxa_list$exclude_subclades_stem}), the stem of the excluded subclade stays available:
the taxon can be sister to the excluded subclade, but never nested within it. A single tip then excludes nothing.
The setting is specific to each excluded subclade, so a taxon can have excluded subclades with different settings.

Excluded subclades are expected to be nested within their target clade. The function checks their position before grafting:
a warning is issued if an excluded subclade is outside the target clade (the exclusion then has no effect).
The function stops if an excluded subclade contains the whole target clade, or is identical to it: no branch (or only the stem branch) would be left for grafting.
To graft taxa specifically on the stem branch of a clade or of a single tip, use \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}} instead.
A warning lists the single-tip excluded subclades with a stem that is not excluded, as they exclude nothing.
}

\subsection{Enclosing clades}{

Under the \code{"BD_clades"} method, when a target clade contains fewer tips than \code{min_tips}, an alternative higher clade is selected to fit the birth-death model.
This 'enclosing clade' is identified by walking rootward the tree node by node, starting from the target clade, until a clade meets the \code{min_tips} condition.
This ensures that the diversification dynamics modeled are based on enough tips to be relevant, while being closely associated with the target clade.
}

\subsection{Methods}{
\itemize{
\item \code{"uniform"}: Taxa are grafted one by one, per target clade from the smallest to the largest, so nested clades are processed first (see below).
Within each target clade, taxa are grafted in random order. The branch used for grafting is drawn among the available branches
with probabilities proportional to branch lengths, and the position on the branch is drawn from a uniform distribution.
This is fast, but does not account for diversification dynamics.
Note that under a birth-death process, the probability of a side lineage splitting off a branch at age t follows the exponential function e^(-rt),
with r the net diversification rate (speciation minus extinction).
A uniform draw implicitly assumes a flat density (r = 0), and gives too much weight to old divergence times compared to the expectation from a birth-death process.
\item \code{"BD_clades"}: Taxa are grafted per target clade, from the smallest to the largest, so nested clades are processed first (see below).
For each target clade, a birth-death model is fitted with \code{phytools::fit.bd()} on the clade, or on the smallest enclosing clade with at least \code{min_tips} tips.
The sampling fraction accounts for the number of missing taxa provided for grafting in this clade.
The divergence times of all taxa missing in the target clade are then jointly drawn with \code{TreeSim::corsim()}, between the present and the stem age of the clade
(or its crown age if the stem is not available for these taxa).
Taxa are grafted from the oldest to the youngest divergence time. Each taxon is grafted on a branch drawn uniformly among the available branches that cross its divergence age.
Younger taxa can therefore be grafted on the branches of older missing taxa. This is the recommended method, but it can be time consuming for large phylogenies.
Taxa of the same target clade with different stem availability are drawn in separate groups, and the birth-death model is fitted only once per target clade.
The group of taxa without access to the stem is grafted first, so these taxa are present when drawing the divergence times of the taxa that can also use the stem
(this is the same logic as for nested clades).
}

With both methods, nested clades are processed first, so the taxa of a larger clade are grafted on a tree that already
includes the taxa of the clades nested within it, and can be grafted on their branches.
For taxa of the same clade, those without access to the stem come first.
For \code{"BD_clades"}, it also ensures that the divergence times of nested clades are accounted for when drawing the divergence times of the larger clade.
Taxa of the same target clade are grafted in random order, so they are exchangeable.
Clades that are not nested do not interact, so the order in which they are processed has no influence.
}

\subsection{Sampling fraction and unsampled species}{

Under \code{method = "BD_clades"}, the birth-death model of a clade is fitted with the sampling fraction
\code{n_present / (n_present + n_pending + n_unsampled)}, with \code{n_present} the tips of the fitted clade in the current tree (backbone tips and taxa already grafted),
\code{n_pending} the taxa of \code{missing_taxa_list} still to graft in this clade, and \code{n_unsampled} the unsampled species derived from \code{total_richness_per_clades}.
Taxa grafted in nested clades processed earlier are already in the tree, so they are not counted as missing anymore.
Unsampled species are only used to fit the rates: they are not grafted, and do not compete with the grafted taxa for the branches.

For a clade with a \code{total_richness} value provided: \code{nb_unsampled = total_richness - nb_backbone - nb_missing}, with \code{nb_missing} the number of taxa to graft
whose target clade is this clade or is nested within it. For a clade with no \code{total_richness} value (\code{NA}, or enclosing a target clade without being listed),
\code{nb_unsampled} is the sum over the largest clades nested within it with a \code{total_richness} value provided.
As such, the unsampled species of nested clades are summed across disjoint clades but never counted twice within nested clades.

If \code{total_richness_per_clades = NULL}, \code{nb_unsampled = 0} and no \code{sampling_fraction_df} is returned.
To obtain the \code{sampling_fraction_df} without informing any total richness, provide \code{total_richness_per_clades} with the clades and only \code{NA} in \verb{$total_richness}:
\code{nb_unsampled = 0} and \code{total_richness} equals the backbone plus missing species in all clades (the sampling fraction is 1).

The function stops, before any grafting, if \code{total_richness_per_clades} does not cover all the target clades, if a \code{total_richness} provided is lower than
the number of backbone species plus species to graft of the clade, or if a clade has fewer unsampled species than the sum of the clades with
a \code{total_richness} provided nested within it.
With \code{method = "uniform"}, no diversification model is used: \code{total_richness_per_clades} is only used to derive the \code{sampling_fraction_df} table (a warning is issued).
}
}
\examples{
# Create a toy backbone phylogeny: ultrametric, with a root age of 50 time units
set.seed(1234)
phy <- ape::rcoal(n = 30)
phy$edge.length <- phy$edge.length / max(ape::node.depth.edgelength(phy)) * 50

# List the missing taxa to graft
missing_taxa_list <- list(
  Taxon_name = c("X1", "X2", "X3", "X4"),
  # Target clades defined by pairs of tips (MRCA), or by a single tip
  # All the other items are named after Taxon_name (same names, same order)
  Target_clade = list(X1 = c("t26", "t18"), X2 = c("t8", "t22"), X3 = "t5", X4 = c("t26", "t18")),
  # Stem of the target clade available for grafting?
  # NA uses the global 'include_target_clade_stem' argument (TRUE by default)
  # X4 cannot be grafted on the stem
  include_target_clade_stem = c(X1 = NA, X2 = NA, X3 = NA, X4 = FALSE),
  # Excluded subclades defined by pairs of tips. Only X1 has an excluded subclade
  # (the optional name of the excluded subclade, here 'clade_A', is used in messages)
  Excluded_subclades = list(X1 = list(clade_A = c("t10", "t19")), X2 = NULL, X3 = NULL, X4 = NULL),
  # Stem of the excluded subclades also excluded?
  # NA / NULL use the global 'exclude_subclades_stem' argument (TRUE by default).
  # Here the stem of the excluded subclade of X1 stays available,
  # so X1 can be sister to it, but never nested within it.
  exclude_subclades_stem = list(X1 = FALSE, X2 = NULL, X3 = NULL, X4 = NULL))

# Graft with the default method ("BD_clades")
grafted_phy <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                  method = "BD_clades", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
plot(grafted_phy, cex = 0.7)

# Color the grafted tips and branches in red based on elements returned with the phylogeny
plot(grafted_phy, cex = 0.7,
     edge.color = c("black", "red")[grafted_phy$grafted_edges + 1],
     tip.color = c("black", "red")[grafted_phy$grafted_tips + 1])

# Graft with the "uniform" method, without using the excluded subclades
grafted_phy_uniform <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                          use_excluded_subclades = FALSE,
                                          method = "uniform", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Check that the constraints are respected
check_grafting_constraints(phy_grafted = grafted_phy, phy_backbone = phy,
                           missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list)

# Account for unsampled species with the total richness of the clades.
# All the unique target clades must be listed
# (NA = unknown richness, i.e., no unsampled species beyond nested clades)
total_richness_per_clades <- list(
  Target_clade = list(c("t26", "t18"), c("t8", "t22"), "t5"),
  total_richness = c(25, NA, 3))
grafted_phy_richness <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                           total_richness_per_clades = total_richness_per_clades,
                                           method = "BD_clades", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
# Sampling fraction of the clades in the grafted phylogeny
grafted_phy_richness$sampling_fraction_df

}
